\title{Complete Execution Traces Degrade SFT-Based Verdict Prediction: \\A Diagnostic Signal Analysis}
\author{Anonymous ACL submission}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Complete execution traces contain all information needed for verdict prediction (classifying whether a test passes or fails), yet they actively impair SFT learning.
Across four code-specialized models (1.5B--13B, three architecture families), complete traces achieve only 77--82\% while compact exception events reach ${\geq}$99.33\%.
A regex exploiting format markers matches this ceiling, but marker removal collapses it to 28.49\% while SFT retains 97.65\%; cross-dataset transfer shows SFT acquires diagnostic patterns beyond format matching.
Gradient masking attributes ${\leq}$4\% of the 18--23pp gap to gradient dilution; content quality at inference explains the majority, though forward-pass attention remains an unquantified residual.
An empirical two-tier signal hierarchy holds across families, yielding a practical strategy: extract exception events first; when unavailable, tail-truncated traces provide a viable fallback.
\end{abstract}

\input{introduction}
\input{method}
\input{experiments}
\input{discussion}
\input{related_work}
\input{conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

Our study has several limitations.
First, all experiments use HumanEval and MBPP-sanitized (591 problems, simple assert-based tests on short Python functions); generalization to human-written code, other languages, non-assert test frameworks (e.g., parametric tests, integration tests, flaky tests), and multi-file projects is unverified.
Second, all four models (1.5B--13B) are code-specialized; models at 30B+ parameters may have sufficient capacity to overcome diagnostic signal dilution, and general-purpose LLMs may behave differently. The gradient masking ablation is validated on two architectures (Qwen-7B, 3 seeds; DeepSeek-6.7B, 1 seed); broader cross-architecture and cross-scale validation remains future work.
Third, multi-seed experiments use only 3 seeds; several baselines (\texttt{no\_trace} for 2/4 models, \texttt{full\_trace} for 2/4 models) rely on single-seed results with Wilson CI variance estimates rather than empirical seed distributions.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work uses publicly available benchmarks (HumanEval, MBPP-sanitized, DebugBench) and does not involve human subjects, crowdsourced annotations, or personally identifiable information.
All models evaluated are open-source and publicly accessible.
Training uses parameter-efficient LoRA fine-tuning on enterprise-grade GPUs, with each experiment using a single GPU.
Total compute is approximately 320 GPU-hours across all conditions, models, seeds, and iterative refinements (including approximately 170 hours for successful experiments; individual runs range from 0.5h for small models to 2.2h for CodeLlama-13B, with the total reflecting 4 models $\times$ multiple conditions $\times$ multiple seeds $\times$ iterative development).
We do not foresee direct negative societal impacts from this work; verdict prediction is a developer-facing tool for automated testing.

